    "lab-2026-11-20-physical-reservoir-narma10": {
      "id": "5f0x77m3p7z161ee8674p1yx1r",
      "created": "2026-11-20T23:45:24Z",
      "hash": "sha256:0d5aa2a5e0d295ba5c5918be7e2606c112c7ccf65a5e86a292386d9c21099783",
      "kind": "thread",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "slug": "lab-2026-11-20-oscillator-reservoir-claim",
          "id": "22v1e3dt4ryr37vxbxcddeevq5",
          "version": 2,
          "html": "<div class=\"blyg-tk-gen\">\n<p><strong>Noisy oscillators fall short of a digital reservoir</strong></p>\n<p>In a simulation, 50 coupled, noisy oscillators acting as a <em>reservoir computer</em> (a system whose own dynamics do the computing, with only a simple trained readout on top) scored an error of 0.96 on NARMA-10, a standard memory test, where 1.0 is no better than always guessing the average. A tuned digital reservoir of the same size scored 0.15.</p>\n<p>How sure: simulation only, one toy model, one task, one range of settings, narrowed after adversarial review. It says nothing about physical reservoirs in general, or about energy. <a href=\"https://github.com/msmsim/kinocomputing-lab/blob/main/experiments/e002-physical-reservoir-narma10/RESULTS.md\">Code and full results</a>.</p>\n</div>"
        }
      ],
