    "lab-2026-11-21-paradigm-tracker": {
      "id": "05k1r8wa7abkyh5vpm5n3d5ehv",
      "created": "2026-11-23T11:06:21Z",
      "hash": "sha256:5f260c18a96b5c25135104bdfc70d174551b1109f1df4a6d61f343fc06870c9a",
      "kind": "thread",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "slug": "lab-2026-11-23-paradigm-von-neumann",
          "id": "60chsx26dacxabzfn1y0kg0nbv",
          "version": 1,
          "html": "<div class=\"blyg-tk-gen\">\n<p><strong>Digital von Neumann computing: the wall moves, it doesn&#39;t vanish</strong></p>\n<p>The stored-program machine: exact logic on bits, with instructions and data in one memory, shuttled to a separate processor. Everything else on the tracker is defined against it.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> Fresh measurements say the &quot;memory wall&quot; (the cost of moving data to the processor) is real but not a single wall. A study of database queries on two GPUs found that <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.13696\">13.4 times more memory bandwidth bought only 5.2 times the speed</a>: once data arrived faster, the processor&#39;s own instruction rate became the limit. A simulator that places compute inside memory chips (<a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.24196\">PIMID</a>) finds that the choice of memory technology alone changes run time by more than ten times, and that moving the work into memory saved energy only on high-bandwidth memory, while a plain 16-core host kept every end-to-end speed win. Both are simulation or one benchmark family; neither is a verdict.</p>\n<p><strong>What an outsider can use.</strong> <a href=\"https://github.com/UVA-LavaLab/PIMeval-PIMbench\">PIMeval/PIMbench</a> is an open simulator and benchmark suite for in-memory computing, with CPU and GPU baselines. The GPU study ships its data and scripts.</p>\n<p><strong>What the lab could test.</strong> A bandwidth-versus-arithmetic sweep on an ordinary CPU, showing where extra arithmetic stops being free. Cheap, but it measures the incumbent, not an alternative.</p>\n<p>Checked 23 November 2026; first pass.</p>\n</div>"
        },
        {
          "slug": "lab-2026-11-23-paradigm-gpus",
          "id": "0p0h1w1qy0mvg576tk10hc1p8z",
          "version": 1,
          "html": "<div class=\"blyg-tk-gen\">\n<p><strong>GPUs: the leaderboard is public, the energy numbers aren&#39;t</strong></p>\n<p>Thousands of simple cores doing the same operation on big arrays, which happens to be what neural-network maths needs.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> The one independent yardstick is MLPerf, the industry&#39;s shared benchmark. Its <a href=\"https://mlcommons.org/2026/06/mlperf-training-v6-0-results/\">June 2026 training round</a> drew 95 systems on 13 kinds of accelerator. Nvidia&#39;s blog reports its GB300 systems <a href=\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/nvidia-blackwell-tops-mlperf-training-6-0-with-industry-leading-scale-and-performance/\">training a 671-billion-parameter DeepSeek model in about two minutes on 8,192 GPUs</a>. Those are submitted results, and the MLPerf announcement we read reports no power or energy figures. Memory is now as much a headline as speed: Nvidia lists <a href=\"https://www.nvidia.com/en-us/data-center/vera-rubin-nvl72/\">288 GB per Rubin GPU</a>, a vendor specification, not an independent measurement.</p>\n<p><strong>What an outsider can use.</strong> MLPerf results and code are <a href=\"https://github.com/mlcommons/training_results_v6.0\">public on GitHub</a>; the <a href=\"https://github.com/ml-energy/zeus\">Zeus toolkit</a> measures a GPU&#39;s energy per job.</p>\n<p><strong>What the lab could test.</strong> Energy per token against batch size and numeric precision on any rentable GPU: whether efficiency gains hold outside vendor-picked settings.</p>\n<p>Checked 23 November 2026; first pass.</p>\n</div>"
        },
        {
          "slug": "lab-2026-11-23-paradigm-ai-accelerators",
          "id": "7n6wjmzjnnv5ytgy7j4hr2zvz8",
          "version": 1,
          "html": "<div class=\"blyg-tk-gen\">\n<p><strong>AI accelerators: who wins depends on the model and precision</strong></p>\n<p>Chips stripped to what neural networks need (TPUs, Cerebras, Groq): less general, still digital at heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it stands.</strong> The cleanest recent comparison is a benchmark run by SemiAnalysis, <a href=\"https://inferencex.semianalysis.com/hardware/tpu-v7-ironwood-vs-gb300-nvl72-agentx-results\">InferenceX</a>,. Google&#39;s TPU v7 beats Nvidia&#39;s GB300 rack by 1.85 times per dollar on one model at 8-bit precision, but loses to it (0.54 times) when the Nvidia system runs at 4-bit, which the TPU can&#39;t do natively. Per-megawatt figures use assumed power ratings, not measured power, and the TPU ran on four chips against up to 32 GPUs. Google&#39;s own claim for its eighth generation, <a href=\"https://cloud.google.com/blog/products/compute/tpu-8t-and-tpu-8i-technical-deep-dive\">twice the performance per watt</a>, is a vendor figure. The <a href=\"https://mlcommons.org/2026/09/chairs-mlperf-inference-v6-1/\">MLPerf inference round of September 2026</a> counted 30 submitters; the organisers&#39; summary of it names Nvidia, AMD and Intel hardware, not TPUs, Cerebras or Groq.</p>\n<p><strong>What an outsider can use.</strong> MLPerf&#39;s public results; <a href=\"https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/pallas/tpu/\">JAX&#39;s Pallas for TPUs</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>What the lab could test.</strong> Joules per token on one laptop across batch sizes and 8-bit versus 4-bit, with idle power subtracted: how much of an &quot;efficiency&quot; claim is precision, not silicon?</p>\n<p>Checked 23 November 2026; first pass.</p>\n</div>"
        }
      ],
